        "text": "Capability Real Windows Simulated Fixture tested Remote access, launch policy Yes (production fleet) — Yes Standard agent: heartbeat, inventory, update scan In production use , standard channel: observed, not a controlled test — Yes Standard (Linux) agent: heartbeat, inventory, pending updates (0.32) No installed Linux endpoint yet The real generated script, run unprivileged on a Linux host against a scratch API, reading that host's real , , and ; dnf and zypper from stubs ( ). Not installed under systemd, not run as root Yes Standard (macOS) agent: heartbeat, inventory, pending updates (0.33) No Mac yet — integration tested only The real generated script against a real API and database, but on Linux: every macOS command ( , , , , , , , , , ) is a stub printing recorded macOS output, and run under dash, and zsh's sh emulation ( , ). Not run on a Mac, under launchd or as root; the installer's macOS steps are only parsed Yes Monitoring collection (services, events, software) No Under pwsh against stubs ( ) Yes ( ) One click diagnostics (the nine catalog checks) No Parsed by PowerShell's parser and run under pwsh against stubs ( ) Yes: real API and database, simulated heartbeat ( ) Signed agent: patch scan, install, restart detection No Yes: runs the agent's real code through — Patch status: restart required No Yes Yes ( ) Patch status: restart completed, confirmed by a later scan No Yes Yes Patch status: failed update (Windows' HRESULT) No Yes Yes Patch status: offline, stale evidence No Yes (scan time moved back 80 hours in the database) Yes Patch status: externally managed (WSUS, Intune) No Yes (the agent's registry detection against a simulated registry) Yes Approved actions (Print Spooler) No Yes ( ) Yes Agent upgrade from the console (0.34): the request, the heartbeat handing exactly that computer its nonce, the served script and its digest pin No — itself has never run on Windows The heartbeat's agent status block under pwsh against fixture files ( ) Yes: real API, real signed repository from , a stand in agent binary ( ) Maintenance actions (service restart, restart schedule/cancel, log collection) No Yes ( ; service manager, , and the session count simulated) Yes ClientSt0r workflow — — Against a contract conformant reference peer ( ). Against the real ClientSt0r: does not interoperate yet ( , four measured gaps) Still needs a lab These are the remaining steps that need hardware or accounts this repository's CI does not have: 1. A disposable Windows 11 client and a Windows Server 2022 on the signed channel, enrolled to a scratch rem0te, each with the five cases set up for real: an update that needs a restart; the same after the restart; an update made to fail (for example, a blocked download host); the machine powered off for 40 hours; one joined to WSUS and one enrolled in Intune with an update ring. 2. A real authenticator on the approval roster, to approve a ring. 3. ClientSt0r , after it adopts ticket rmm v1 (CLIENTST0R.md), paired with a scratch rem0te: 's scenarios repeated with ClientSt0r in place of the reference peer. 4. A disposable Debian or Ubuntu VM and a Fedora or openSUSE VM , installed from a scratch rem0te's Linux installer as root, so the systemd timer, the 0600 secret and run for real. 5. A Mac (Apple silicon, and ideally an Intel one with Homebrew) installed from a scratch rem0te's macOS installer with , so the LaunchDaemon, the root only secret, the migration of a pre 0.33 secret, , and the real , , , and output are exercised. Until then the recorded output the tests use is the only evidence of those formats. Until those have been run, the \"Real Windows\" column above stays No , and the Linux and macOS agents are integration tested only."
