        "heading": "Monitoring and alerts",
        "id": "",
        "text": "Monitoring and alerts Since 0.29, Rem0te watches the computers it manages and raises an alert when something needs a person: a computer offline, a disk nearly full, a Windows service stopped, matching events in an event log, a restart pending for days. Alerts are deduplicated, a site wide outage is recognised as one problem, alerts can be held for maintenance, and unacknowledged alerts escalate to further destinations — including a ticket in ClientSt0r. Monitoring only reads. A monitor never changes a computer. Everything it uses is something the agent already reports: the heartbeat (CPU, memory, disk, restart pending), the inventory, and a small monitor report listing the services and event queries the server asked about. Monitors judge each reading as it arrives. The same readings are also kept over time (0.33) and charted on a computer's Performance tab — see metric history.md. History raises no alerts, and monitoring does not read it. 1. Alerts in the sidebar — shown to anyone with . 2. Totals for what is open now, including how many are held by a maintenance window. 3. Filter by state and severity. The default is open and acknowledged. 4. A site outage lists the computers it gathered; they are not notified on their own while it is open. 5. Acknowledge stops escalation; Resolve needs a note."
